From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:34:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: add a Facebook Messenger protocol (prpl-gometa) to the
 plugin

* Add a Facebook Messenger protocol (prpl-gometa) to the plugin

Register a second prpl, prpl-gometa ("Facebook (E2EE)"), from the same
shared object. It talks to Messenger through messagix
(go.mau.fi/mautrix-meta/pkg/messagix) and handles end-to-end encrypted
threads through whatsmeow's Facebook (armadillo) client. It has to live
in this plugin rather than in one of its own: whatsmeow and messagix are
both Go, and two Go c-archives cannot share one process, so both
protocols run on this plugin's Go runtime. All of its identifiers are
prefixed gometa to stay clear of the WhatsApp code.

- gometa.go: login with e-mail, password and an interactive second
  factor (the session is cached in the account), contact and thread
  sync, incoming messages, plaintext sends, presence
- gometa_e2ee.go: E2EE threads - registration, sending, reactions and
  receipts; a plaintext send that Facebook does not confirm is retried
  over E2EE and the thread remembered as encrypted
- gometa_media.go, gometa_video.go: pictures, voice notes, video and
  documents, plaintext and E2EE
- gometabridge.[ch], glue/gometa_init.c: the C side and the prpl
  registration
- incoming E2EE media is downloaded through the Facebook client
  (fbDownloadable) and shown like WhatsApp media; download_attachment()
  now recovers from a panic, which would otherwise abort the whole
  process from inside a cgo call
- the account-removed and send-reaction handlers of the webOS
  integration also serve prpl-gometa accounts, and deleting one removes
  its gometa-e2ee-<user>.db

go.mod pins mautrix-meta v0.2607.0 and mirrors the beeper/req replace
that its messagix package needs. On top of 1.27.0's whatsmeow, mautrix
moves to v0.32.0 (v0.29.0 does not build against go.mau.fi/util v0.11.0)
and the beeper/req replace moves to v3.0.0-20260925135712-b49593750e56
(the older one does not build against quic-go v0.61.0). mautrix-meta
v0.2608.0 and later rename the messagix events and split
types.MessengerLite, and are not used yet.

* Keep a Facebook login failure from taking the service down

Signing in to Facebook crashed imlibpurpleservice, and with it every
other account, over and over until systemd gave up. mautrix-meta
v0.2607.0 dereferences a nil literal in the bloks interpreter
(bk.action.f32.Eq on a missing argument) during the login flow, and a
panic in the login goroutine ends the whole process, since the Go
runtime is shared with WhatsApp.

Move to mautrix-meta v0.2610.0, which carries the upstream fix
(bd10396, an absent argument evaluates to null) and port to its event
API: ConnectedEvent, ReconnectedEvent, TransientDisconnectEvent,
PermanentErrorEvent, and table updates delivered as *table.LSTable.
MessengerLite is now MessengerLiteIOS.

Run the login under a recover as well, so a failure we have not met
yet signs that one account out with an error instead of killing the
service.

* Show received Facebook pictures, files, stickers and locations

An encrypted Facebook chat showed pictures, videos, voice messages and
files, but dropped a sticker, a location and a text with a link
preview: only a plain text was read. A plain chat showed nothing but
text: a message with no text of its own was dropped, so its picture,
file, sticker or location never showed, and a picture's caption came
without the picture.

Encrypted chats: a sticker is downloaded like a picture and kept as
<hash>.webp, as a received WhatsApp sticker is. A location, a live
location and a location shared as "extended content" become the text
WhatsApp posts for one, whose "geo:" line the Messaging app shows as
its location chip. A text with a link preview shows the preview's
title and description above the text. A picture keeps its caption.

Plain chats: a message's blob, sticker and extended ("XMA")
attachments are gathered by message id. A file is fetched from
Facebook's CDN on its own goroutine and posted as WhatsApp's media is,
one file:// URL per line under /media/internal/.im-attachments/facebook
and the text after them; it is named after its fbid, so the same file
in the next login's inbox is not fetched again. A location becomes the
WhatsApp location text, another shared link its title and link, and
the like sticker a thumbs-up, as mautrix-meta shows it.

What the handler shows, and what it asks of the two clients, now goes
through two small interfaces, so tests can stand in for libpurple and
for Facebook.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [LuneOS addition, not part of purple-gowhatsapp]
